      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274301/assets/fbfe241043e9014c1b633d163cf7e06234db70702a9052297b5df8b9df3fd82f.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274301/assets/d432857e3350dfcb70a2a00b66ad9b86bc207382edd277c2169be0834c1710e0.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={y}^{2}={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}\\frac{1}{t}+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t){|}_{1/2}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
